{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And you shall hang him</b>. Our Rabbis said (Sanhedrin 41a): all who are stoned are hanged. And the meaning of what is stated, <b>for a curse of Elohim is he who is hanged</b>, is an affront to the King, for man is made in the likeness of the King's image, and Israel are His sons — a parable of two brothers who resemble one another, etc.; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:23). And <b>all who are stoned are hanged</b> is the opinion of an individual, and the halakhah follows the Sages (there) that he is hanged only in the case of the blasphemer and the idol worshipper. And the reason for (Deuteronomy 21:23) <b>for a curse of Elohim is he who is hanged</b> is that people will say: why is this one hanged? Because he blessed [i.e., cursed] the Name, or because he worshipped idolatry — a certain one, in such and such a manner of worship, and such and such a sign and portent was seen in it. And the account told of it and of its worship will be called קללה (a curse). Or it is an expression of contempt, as the Rav mentioned, from (I Kings 2:8) <b>and he cursed me a fierce curse</b>, (I Samuel 3:13) <b>for his sons were cursing themselves</b> — meaning, they held in contempt. And the parable of the twin brothers has a secret meaning; it is not as the Rav thought, concerning Israel who are called sons of the Place. \n\n And by way of the plain sense he would say: when there is in a man a grave sin that warrants execution and hanging upon a tree for the greatness of his sin, even so you shall not leave his corpse hanging on the tree, for the accursed and reviled among all people is the hanged one — there is no death as repulsive and disgraceful as this one, and it is not fitting that we defile the land and that the curse of Elohim be in the Holy Land, for there He ordained blessing, life eternal. Therefore Joshua commanded (Joshua 10:26), <b>and they took them down from the trees</b>. \n\n And in my opinion, the matter of the sons of Saul who were impaled (II Samuel 21) was because they were not hanged by a court of Israel and not by the hand of Israel at all; rather, David gave them to the Gibeonites to do with them as they wished, and it was they who impaled them and refused to bury them, in order to publicize their vengeance against them, so that the rest would hear and fear. And when water was poured upon them from the heavens, then David knew that their sin had been forgiven and Elohim had answered the land, for HaShem had attended to the land with rain and the famine ceased, and then he commanded and they buried them with their forefathers — for the honor of the kingdom, not as an obligation at all, for we were commanded only that we not defile the land with one who has been hanged. And behold, HaShem did not accept their sin through their hanging immediately, to make known that HaShem loves converts, as the words of our Rabbis (Yevamot 79a). \n\n And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 21:23) by way of the plain sense, that אלהים is an active noun [i.e., an agent of Elohim], and the curse will come upon a place near the hanged one, and it has a secret attached to the soul; therefore you shall not defile your land. Now, one who leaves [a corpse] hanging on the tree transgresses a negative commandment and a positive commandment. And our Rabbis (Sanhedrin 46b) expounded thus concerning all who leave their dead without honor: just as a tree is repulsive [when it had previously been used for a corpse], so too all [such persons] are repulsive. And according to their opinion, <b>for a curse of Elohim is he who is hanged</b> comes to say: even though this one deserves to be degraded for the greatness of his sin, you shall not do so, for a curse of Elohim is he who is hanged. And according to the parable of the two brothers, one who leaves [the corpse hanging] would fall under this reasoning, and likewise the curse that Rabbi Abraham mentioned would apply in every house where there is a dead person, and therefore (Numbers 19:14) every person who comes into the tent and everything that is in the tent becomes impure."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "I read והמלין / המלין throughout as referring to המלין (one who leaves [the corpse hanging]), a usage Ramban derives from the rabbinic derivation at Sanhedrin 46b; the word may alternatively point to one who neglects burial. The phrase מן המלין in the manuscript possibly underlies the repetition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:25.844826263Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}